::ENG1_U04_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.W.03] Prompt: Source finding: Some students with quiet study access reported fewer homework distractions. Which publication-ready sentence is most accurate?
{
~A. Quiet rooms always make every student finish all homework. #Incorrect. This exaggerates the source finding and is not publication-ready.
~B. Quiet rooms are perfect for everybody forever. #Incorrect. This is exaggerated and not source-based.
~C. Homework and rooms are school things. #Incorrect. This is too vague and does not communicate the source finding.
=D. A student survey suggests that quiet study access may reduce homework distractions for some students. #Correct. This keeps the finding accurate and avoids exaggeration.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Audience: ninth-grade students. Topic: online calendars. Which heading best fits?
{
=A. How Online Calendars Help Students Track Due Dates. #Correct. This heading is clear, specific, and useful for the audience.
~B. Calendar Stuff. #Incorrect. This heading is vague and informal for a student-facing publication.
~C. A Long History of Paper Planners. #Incorrect. This does not match the online calendar topic.
~D. Things You Might Know. #Incorrect. This does not guide the reader.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Which checklist item best protects source accuracy before publication?
{
~A. Make sure the colors are bright. #Incorrect. Color choice does not protect research accuracy.
=B. Check that each source finding is stated without exaggeration. #Correct. Publication-ready work must preserve source meaning accurately.
~C. Make sure the title is large. #Incorrect. A large title does not verify source information.
~D. Remove all source references. #Incorrect. Removing source context weakens source-based communication.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Which product choice best fits a student audience that needs quick information?
{
~A. A dense page with no headings. #Incorrect. This is hard for students to scan.
=B. A one-page digital summary with headings and brief source-based explanations. #Correct. This format supports readability and audience fit.
~C. A private draft no reader can access. #Incorrect. A publication must be prepared for an audience.
~D. A random list of copied facts. #Incorrect. Source-based publication needs organization and accuracy.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Prompt: Which sentence is publication-ready?
{
~A. The source say calendars helps students. #Incorrect. The sentence has grammar errors that make it unready for publication.
~B. Calendar thing helps kids do stuff. #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
=C. The source suggests that online calendars may help students track due dates. #Correct. This sentence uses standard English and accurate source language.
~D. Students tracks due dates good. #Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}
